WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.860
INTRO:
Just 8 years ago, Exxon was the most valuable

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:07.799
company in the world boasting a peak market
cap of $446 billion.

00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:10.640
But since then, Exxon has slowly slipped down
the list.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.610
First, they lost the number 1 spot, then they
dropped out of the top 10, and now they’ve

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:20.519
tumbled all the way down to number 33 with
a market cap of $259 billion.

00:00:20.519 --> 00:00:26.150
That means that Exxon is officially over ten
times behind the new market leader Apple.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:30.630
To make things worse, Exxon has struggled
to regain profitability over the past year,

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:34.620
and to top it all off, Exxon was kicked out
of the Dow Jones last year.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.230
The Dow Jones is a combination of the top
30 American companies that hit a set of guidelines,

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:43.080
and Exxon had been part of this list for 92
years.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:48.190
Yet, it looks like hitting a 100 years in
the Dow simply wasn’t in the cards for Exxon.

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:52.630
At first glance, it’s easy to attribute
this fall to the rise of EVs, but EVs only

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:54.920
account for 3% of global car sales.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:59.400
So, even though an EV takeover is inevitable,
EVs aren’t really the ones eating into Exxon’s

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.440
top and bottom lines today.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.940
In fact, Exxon would likely be in a very similar
situation even if it wasn’t for EVs, so

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:11.950
here’s what happened to the oil king that
dominated the businessworld for nearly a century.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:17.190
FALLING OIL PRICES:
Taking a look back, the most obvious factor

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:20.650
behind Exxon’s fall is of course crashing
oil prices.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:24.460
When your entire business is based on oil,
you’re basically screwed if oil takes a

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:29.220
nosedive, and that’s exactly what happened
between 2014 and 2016.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.580
Within those two years, oil prices plummeted
from $120 per barrel down to just $40 per

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:35.580
barrel.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:39.400
This flash crash was caused by a handful of
factors most of which Exxon had no control

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.400
over.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.550
First of all, the US dollar was popping off
against the Euro, and given that commodities

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:48.690
are generally quoted in USD, a strengthening
dollar meant falling commodity prices.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:53.250
In the meantime, China was actively trying
to do the exact opposite by devaluing the

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:54.830
yuan in 2015.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:02:00.350
You see, China likes to maintain a weak currency
on purpose, and while that might sound stupid,

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.760
there is reason behind the madness.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.360
When you have a weak currency, it’s easier
for foreigners to afford your exports which

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.149
increases exports.

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:12.420
At the same time, it also becomes harder for
citizens to afford imported items which reduces

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:13.610
overall imports.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:18.550
While this is a good thing for China, this
was terrible for Exxon given that China is

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:20.840
the world’s largest oil importer.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.940
All of a sudden, Exxon had substantially less
revenue from Americans and the Chinese, and

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:29.709
to make things worse, the middle eastern oil
giants would screw over Exxon as well.

00:02:29.709 --> 00:02:33.260
Given the reduced demand for oil, it would’ve
been a smart move for OPEC to decrease oil

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:34.260
production.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:38.000
Now, a lot of you would argue that decreasing
production is an unethical way to prop up

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.470
prices, but from their side it would’ve
been smart.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:46.220
But, they decided not to reduce oil production
which led to a surplus in oil which further

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:47.880
drove down prices.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.370
And the nail in the coffin was the Iran nuclear
deal which allowed Iran to start exporting

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:52.470
oil again.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:57.040
It’s not clear how much of an impact this
additional supply actually had, but the threat

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.680
itself was enough for investors to flee oil.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:06.090
All of these factors combined led to Exxon’s
revenue crashing over 50% from $440 billion

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.110
down to $205 billion.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:12.239
But falling oil prices was just a taste of
what was to come.

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:16.319
NEGATIVE OIL PRICES:
Usually, with any investment, the worst case

00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:19.270
scenario is just losing everything you put
in.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:22.440
Unless you’re shorting a stock, the worst
case scenario is just 0.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:28.510
But, with oil prices things went beyond zero
when oil prices literally went negative.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:32.250
No one really expected how quickly the pandemic
could shut down the world and decrease oil

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:33.530
demand to nearly 0.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:37.670
So, it’s not surprising that producers and
refiners were left with large amounts of excess

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:39.660
oil once the pandemic hit.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:43.300
The problem though is that you can’t just
throw the oil into some reserves and try to

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.560
sell it once demand recovers.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.340
Oil giants already had hundreds of tankers
carrying millions of barrels worth oil to

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:50.340
their destinations.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:55.060
But once they reached their destination, customers
no longer wanted the oil, so they literally

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:58.930
had millions of barrels of oil just floating
around in the ocean doing nothing.

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:03.260
And every day these tankers were just sitting
around, loads of money was being burned.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:06.790
It was just a matter of time until someone
broke and paid someone to simply take away

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:10.980
the oil, and that’s exactly what happened
on April 20, 2020.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:16.739
In just one day, crude oil futures fell 306%
from $18.27 to negative $37.63.

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:22.430
And just when you think things can’t get
any worse, Saudi Arabia and Russia decide

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:23.919
to have a price war.

00:04:23.919 --> 00:04:27.710
Common sense would tell you that when oil
prices are plummeting and even going negative,

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:31.150
it’s a good time to heavily reduce or even
halt oil production.

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:35.169
Yet, Saudi Arabia and Russia decided to do
the exact opposite.

00:04:35.169 --> 00:04:38.660
Both of them knew that the other was losing
money, and so, they tried to basically run

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:39.860
each other out of business.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:44.379
Eventually, they did come to a truce, but
not before pumping up OPEC oil output to 30

00:04:44.379 --> 00:04:47.379
year highs when prices were literally negative.

00:04:47.379 --> 00:04:52.009
Anyway, Exxon wasn’t the leading voice behind
the 2014 oil crash, the pandemic, or negative

00:04:52.009 --> 00:04:55.210
oil prices, but they got obliterated in the
crossfire.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:59.629
Now, nothing we’ve discussed so far is specific
to Exxon, and all oil companies were hurt

00:04:59.629 --> 00:05:00.770
by these events.

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:05.270
But unlike Exxon and Shell, oil companies
like Chevron have almost fully recovered.

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:09.770
In fact, Chevron is only 10 to 15% away from
all time highs, and they never got kicked

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:10.770
from the Dow Jones.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:15.599
So, clearly, a lot of the blame has to be
placed on Exxon as well.

00:05:15.599 --> 00:05:19.749
UNWISE INVESTMENTS:
Probably the easiest loss Exxon could’ve

00:05:19.749 --> 00:05:23.150
avoided was making outsized bets on the oil
industry.

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:26.730
Even though EVs haven’t made a big dent
in the overall oil industry yet, it’s just

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:30.949
a matter of time, and investing heavily into
oil ventures is plain stupid.

00:05:30.949 --> 00:05:34.349
But, that’s exactly what Exxon has been
doing over the past decade.

00:05:34.349 --> 00:05:38.319
In 2009, they spent $41 billion buying XTO
energy.

00:05:38.319 --> 00:05:42.289
In 2012, they spent $2.6 billion buying Celtic
Exploration.

00:05:42.289 --> 00:05:46.719
In 2017, they spent another $2.5 billion acquiring
InterOil.

00:05:46.719 --> 00:05:50.369
And that same year, they spent another $6.6
billion to buy the Bass Family’s Permian

00:05:50.369 --> 00:05:51.539
assets.

00:05:51.539 --> 00:05:55.469
Aside from making acquisitions left and right,
Exxon has also been investing heavily into

00:05:55.469 --> 00:05:57.129
their own business.

00:05:57.129 --> 00:06:01.309
As recent as January 2020, the CEO of Exxon,
Darren Woods, announced that Exxon would be

00:06:01.309 --> 00:06:05.020
investing $30 billion into exploration projects
in 2020 alone.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:09.309
And the plan was to spend $30 to $35 billion
every year through 2025.

00:06:09.309 --> 00:06:12.749
Unfortunately for Exxon, all of these poor
investments are only starting to bite back

00:06:12.749 --> 00:06:14.050
after a decade.

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:18.039
For the longest time, Exxon didn’t want
to admit that these were bad investments.

00:06:18.039 --> 00:06:22.219
They insisted that these investments just
needed time to recover, and they refused to

00:06:22.219 --> 00:06:24.499
value these assets below what they paid for
them.

00:06:24.499 --> 00:06:28.650
But recently, Exxon has been getting sued
for overvaluing their assets and misleading

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:29.800
investors.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:34.139
Exxon finally gave in at the end of 2020,
and wrote off the $41 billion XTO investment

00:06:34.139 --> 00:06:36.110
as a $20 billion loss.

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:39.809
And this right here is what really killed
Exxon in 2020.

00:06:39.809 --> 00:06:43.499
Even during the height of the pandemic, Exon
was only losing half a billion to $1 billion

00:06:43.499 --> 00:06:44.619
per quarter.

00:06:44.619 --> 00:06:49.650
But after writing off this loss in the last
quarter of 2020, Exxon posted a $20 billion

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:50.650
loss.

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:54.129
And that marked the end of Exxons first unprofitable
year in 40 years.

00:06:54.129 --> 00:06:58.580
Exxon isn’t in the clear yet either, there’s
still multiple ongoing lawsuits accusing Exxon

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:00.189
of overvaluing their other assets.

00:07:00.189 --> 00:07:05.960
So, last year’s painful write down may just
be the first of many.

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:10.340
DIVIDEND LEGACY:
Moving onto Exxon’s other big money drain,

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:12.240
we have their dividend payments.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.719
One of the defining characteristics of Exxon
stock is their flawless history of dividend

00:07:15.719 --> 00:07:17.159
payments.

00:07:17.159 --> 00:07:20.539
Exxon has literally never missed a dividend
payment in their history.

00:07:20.539 --> 00:07:25.229
Standard Oil began paying a dividend in 1882,
and Exxon has kept up the tradition ever since

00:07:25.229 --> 00:07:26.370
the break up.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:29.869
This means that Exxon didn’t miss a payment
through world war 1, the great depression,

00:07:29.869 --> 00:07:34.589
world war 2, the inflation crisis, the 1973
oil crisis, the dotcom bubble, the financial

00:07:34.589 --> 00:07:37.250
recession, and of course the pandemic.

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:41.409
This places Exxon in an elite group of stocks
called dividend aristocrats who not only never

00:07:41.409 --> 00:07:44.129
miss a payment, but continuously increase
their payments.

00:07:44.129 --> 00:07:49.300
It’s not always super easy to pay a dividend
though especially a high yielding dividend.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:53.259
Over the past few years, Exxon has been forced
to borrow money to pay out their dividends,

00:07:53.259 --> 00:07:57.120
and while that’s super irresponsible, at
this point, a lot of Exxon’s leadership

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:59.719
would rather file for bankruptcy than miss
a dividend payment.

00:07:59.719 --> 00:08:01.229
And I don’t blame them.

00:08:01.229 --> 00:08:05.559
If they missed a dividend payment, not only
would they ruin a 140 year streak that’s

00:08:05.559 --> 00:08:09.279
virtually impossible to rebuild, but they
would give up one of the only things that’s

00:08:09.279 --> 00:08:10.789
still going for Exxon.

00:08:10.789 --> 00:08:15.349
Exxon may be going bankrupt over the next
decade, but one thing many investors are still

00:08:15.349 --> 00:08:19.069
extremely confident in is that Exxon will
pay dividends till the very end.

00:08:19.069 --> 00:08:22.499
And if Exxon were to miss a payment right
now, that would simply destroy investor trust

00:08:22.499 --> 00:08:25.889
and drive the stock price even lower, at least
in the short term.

00:08:25.889 --> 00:08:29.499
With that being said though, paying out a
massive dividend given the company’s situation

00:08:29.499 --> 00:08:34.710
is extremely irresponsible, and it’s objectively
holding back the company.

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:38.560
BLEAK FUTURE:
Looking into the future, it’s not clear

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:39.820
where Exxon is headed.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:44.280
In terms of financials, Exxon is nearly back
to pre-pandemic profit levels on a quarterly

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:45.280
basis.

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:48.550
But, the thing to keep in mind is that the
pandemic wasn’t what hurt Exxon the most,

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:52.070
it was actually writing down that $20 billion
investment loss.

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:55.700
And given that Exxon has plenty of other such
investments, it’s very possible that they

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:58.150
continue to incur such losses over the next
decade.

00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:01.900
In the meantime, Exxon refuses to embrace
renewable energy.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:04.950
Exxon is actually currently being investigated
for spreading disinformation regarding climate

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:07.210
change and downplaying its effect.

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:10.840
When questioned in court, the CEO, Darren
Woods, of course denied these allegations.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:14.600
But whether Exxon was spreading disinformation
or not, their stance on the future is crystal

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:15.600
clear.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:18.940
Darren Woods has repeatedly reiterated that
climate change does not pose any long term

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:20.740
threats to the oil industry.

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:25.100
In my opinion, this viewpoint is fundamentally
flawed as the transition to renewable energy

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:26.720
has little to do with climate change.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:30.580
Now, don’t get me wrong here, switching
to renewable energy will help with combating

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:34.860
climate change, but climate change is not
what’s pushing the transition itself.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:38.340
The primary factor that will move the needle
in my opinion is electric options being more

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:42.190
convenient, easier to maintain, and most importantly
cheaper.

00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:45.770
Once these demands are met, companies and
people will switch to renewable options regardless

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:47.371
of whether they believe in climate change
or not.

00:09:47.371 --> 00:09:51.220
Now, it’s not like this change is gonna
happen overnight and Exxon is about to go

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:52.600
bankrupt next year.

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:56.400
But, the longer Exxon chooses to ignore this
trend, the more likely it is that they’ll

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:57.400
end up like Texaco.

00:09:57.400 --> 00:09:59.400
But that’s just what I think.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.020
Do you guys think Exxon has a future?

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:02.370
Comment that down below.

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:06.670
Also, drop a like if you’re glad that the
era of oil giants is coming to an end.

